{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZXyhaLQJwkD4wLPYVCi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 02 (the brute-force checker) is done, and the gate passes: `lake build` is OK, the sorry count is unchanged at 1, and 1,231 tests pass. Nothing is committed.\n\n**What I built**\n- **`paper2/complex_check.py`**: runs as `python -m paper2.complex_check` and takes about 2.5 minutes on 32 cores. It computes every Table 1 quantity from its source's own definition, never through pathwidth:\n  - pathwidth by searching bag sequences;\n  - vertex separation, Lengauer's vertex separator game, narrowness, cutwidth and modified cutwidth as minima over all orderings;\n  - interval thickness by two independent interval-model searches, which agree on every graph on up to 6 vertices;\n  - node and edge search as game-state searches with recontamination, plus the monotone versions;\n  - interval bandwidth over Fomin's numberings of any length;\n  - MOSP, gate matrix layout, PLA folding and one-dimensional logic by column order plus an explicit track assignment.\n- **`tests/test_complex_check.py`**: 14 tests on graphs checked by hand (path, star, cycle, K4, K3,3, edgeless graphs, the identity matrix, Ohtsuki instances).\n- **Inputs:** all 1,252 graphs on 1–7 vertices (networkx atlas), 400 random graphs on 7–8 vertices, 2,130 matrices (every class up to 4×4 plus 1,500 random 5×5), and 1,027 random boundary-gate instances.\n\n**Results**\n- **No failures on any exact statement from item 01.** That covers MOSP = gate matrix layout = pathwidth + 1, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness, node search = interval thickness = vertex separation + 1 (on graphs with an edge), node search = 0 on edgeless graphs, vertex separation = pathwidth, and Lengauer's game = vertex separation. It also covers Lengauer's Theorems 4 and 7, Ellis–Sudborough–Turner's Theorem 2.2, and the edge-search and interval-bandwidth bands.\n- **The bands are real.** Edge search minus vertex separation takes all three values 0, 1 and 2. Interval bandwidth minus pathwidth takes both 0 and 1.\n- **Monotone games match the full games on every graph checked**, so recontamination never helped at these sizes.\n- **Item 01's counterexamples hold:**\n  - Cutwidth is outside ±1 of pathwidth + 1 on 341 of 1,644 graphs, and modified cutwidth on 738.\n  - PLA folding of the 5×5 identity needs 3 tracks against 1.\n\n**One item-01 verdict was wrong, and I corrected it.** Item 01's boundary-gate example for one-dimensional logic (the `kK_2` family) misread Ohtsuki's connection graph. His eq. (6) builds it over all gates, boundary gates included, and on that graph the family has no gap. The boundary variant does have a real gap: tracks are pathwidth + 1 or pathwidth + 2 on everything checked, with an explicit 5-net path instance showing the +1. That makes it a band, not a fixed offset. Whether the gap can ever reach 2 is still open.\n\n**Limits**, all recorded in `paper2/equivalences.md`:\n- Split bandwidth is computed directly from node splittings only on six small graphs. At scale the check goes through interval bandwidth and relies on Fomin's Theorem 6 (split bandwidth = interval bandwidth).\n- Lengauer's Theorem 7 is checked only for graphs up to 4 vertices, and edge search only up to 20 edges.\n\n`paper2/equivalences.md` now has the Checked column filled in, the corrected §3, and a new section \"Item 02: the brute-force check\" with the full results table. PROGRESS.md says 2/14 SOLVED and item 02 is marked `[x]`. Twelve items remain, so the loop is not complete."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1688,"cache_read_input_tokens":176647,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1688,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":801,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"di